    "connect": "I have all five files. Here is the synthesis.\n\n---\n\n## Synthesis: The primorial-killer mechanism and the existing LRC threads\n\n### (1) Yes — the primorial killer IS the \"kill the clock\" / apex-lift mechanism made global\n\nThe new finding's killer speed `a(k-1)`, chosen divisible by every `d | (k-1)`, is precisely the **\"multiple-of-n apex row\"** that HYP-2101 and HYP-2102 already isolated as the positive-measure side of the apex seam — but read in the *opposite* direction.\n\n- HYP-2101's clock ledger keeps an **n-clock floor seam** witness at `t = j/n` (here `t = j/d` for the coarse tight times). Its \"kill the clock\" intuition: a speed that is `0 mod n` (an apex/multiple-of-n runner) is *invisible* at the floor times `j/n` because `||(mult of n)·(j/n)|| = 0`. HYP-2101 flagged exactly these rows (\"bounded rows containing a multiple of `n` were all loose/positive-measure for n=4,6,8,10,12,14\") and HYP-2102 noted \"a crude measure lower bound failed.\"\n- The new finding **explains why** those rows are loose and **turns the loss into a gain**: a single killer `a(k-1)` divisible by *every* `d | (k-1)` annihilates *all* the coarse tight times `t = j/d` simultaneously (it sits at distance 0 from every one of them). With all coarse danger-times killed, the surviving minimum is forced down onto a *finer* clock, landing at the below-mediant level `a/(a(k+1)-1)` rather than the generic `2/(2k+1)`.\n\nSo the apex-lift sheaf's \"preserves a cheap pair / creates a new pair-sum witness / opens positive measure\" trichotomy (Candidate Theorem 1) has a fourth, previously-uncatalogued outcome on the multiple-of-n rows: **the lift can DEEPEN the floor** when the lifted speed is a primorial multiple that kills a whole tower of coarse clocks at once. The depth `a` is exactly the number of distinct prime clocks the single killer can switch off — which is why `a ~ ω(k-1)`. This is the literal content behind HYP-2101's metaphor that nonunit shells \"are places where a visible section may need to be lifted or transferred\" — here the transfer is from clock `d` down to a finer clock, and the killer is the transfer agent.\n\nThe connection corrects the apex thread's framing: the apex/multiple-of-n runner is not merely a positive-measure *obstruction* to gluing; on the primorial subsequence it is an active *clock-killer* that produces genuine below-edge configs.\n\n### (2) `a_max` unbounded says nothing dangerous for LRC(14)=k=13 — the floor there is shallow and finite\n\nFor k=13: `k-1 = 12 = 2^2·3`, so `ω(12) = 2`, giving `a ≤ 3`. The primorial level reached is\n\n  `level a=3:  M = 3/(3·14 − 1) = 3/41`,\n\nwhich is exactly the value the prompt reports finding. Key implications:\n\n- **The gap does NOT close to the floor at k=13.** `a_max` unbounded is an *asymptotic* statement realized only along the sparse subsequence where `k-1` is primorial (`k-1` divisible by the first `a−1` primes: 2, 6, 30, 210, ...). k=13 sits at the very shallow end (`a=3`), so 3/41 is the deepest below-mediant value, still comfortably above the floor `1/14`:\n\n  `1/14 ≈ 0.0714  <  3/41 ≈ 0.0732  <  2/27 ≈ 0.0741` (the generic doubled-apex edge).\n\n  The below-mediant config dips only into the thin band `(1/14, 2/27)` — the very interval HYP-2052/oracle-S552 wrongly asserted was empty. This is the local incarnation of the S573 correction (HYP-2084): the global \"no config in (1/n, 2/(2n−1))\" claim is false, and 3/41 at k=13 is the concrete LRC(14) witness of that.\n\n- **This is good news for the LRC(14) proof program.** Because `a ≤ 3` is finite and tiny, the second-spectrum structure below the edge at k=13 is a *finite, enumerable* family of clock-depths (d | 12 ∈ {1,2,3,4,6,12}, killer ≡ 0 mod lcm of a chosen subset). The unbounded `a_max` is a phenomenon of *other* k (e.g. k−1 = 30, 210); it never reaches LRC(14). So the THM-534 / sheaf machinery for n=14 only has to clear finitely many below-edge depths, not an unbounded tower.\n\n### (3) The moment-LP dual (THM-534) is fully consistent with below-mediant configs — it bounds a different quantity and never used the empty-gap claim\n\nTHM-534 is consistent, for three independent reasons:\n\n- **It bounds `meas(S7(E))`, not `M(S)`.** The dual certificate `meas(S7(E)) ≤ L_y(E) = Σ_r y_r S_r(E)` is a *measure* bound on the seven-sector cover set at the mod-7 tie-wall (n=14). It is a per-E Bonferroni inequality from integer-root nonnegativity of `g(t)` on `{0,...,6}`. Nothing in its proof asserts a spectral gap or an empty interval `(1/n, 2/(2n−1))`; it never relied on HYP-2052's (now-corrected) claim.\n\n- **The certificate is a one-sided cap, so below-mediant configs only make it *easier*, not harder.** THM-534 proves `meas(S7(E)) ≤ L_y(E)` for **every** E and certifies `L_y(consec_k) ≤ cap_k`. A below-mediant config (like the 3/41 killer set) is exactly a config whose collar is *small* — i.e. it is *farther* from violating LRC, hence lives well inside the safe side of the cap. The dangerous direction for THM-534 is the AP / consec (the floor-tight maximizer of `L_y`), not the primorial killers. Below-mediant sets sit at large cap-slack, the regime where THM-534 notes consec \"does NOT maximize L_y but HARMLESSLY.\"\n\n- **The open crux is untouched and even clarified.** THM-534's remaining piece is the scalar extremality \"`consec maximizes L_y(E)`.\" The primorial finding says the *non-consec* below-edge configs are primorial-killer sets concentrated at coarse clocks `t = j/d`. Their factorial moments `S_r` are governed by how many sectors a single multiple-of-d killer leaves unhit — a structured, low-rank perturbation. This is consistent with consec maximizing `L_y` (consec minimizes single-miss `S_1`, maximizes `S_2,S_4`), because a primorial killer set, by collapsing onto a coarse clock, *increases* `S_1` (leaves whole sectors empty at the killed times) — pushing `L_y` *down*, away from the consec maximum. So the below-mediant family is exactly the kind of config the THM-534 extremality lemma predicts should be sub-maximal.\n\n### One-line bridge across all five files\n\nThe primorial-killer family unifies the threads: it is the **explicit witness** (HYP-2084/S573) that fills the supposedly-empty gap of HYP-2052/oracle-S552; it is the **deepening outcome of the multiple-of-n apex lift** that HYP-2101's sheaf flagged as positive-measure but never identified as a clock-killer; its depth `a ~ ω(k−1)` is **unbounded asymptotically but caps at a=3 (value 3/41) for LRC(14)**, leaving the n=14 below-edge structure finite; and it is **orthogonal to and consistent with THM-534**, which bounds the mod-7 cover measure on the consec/AP side where killer sets are harmless.\n\n**Files referenced (all read-only, absolute paths):**\n- `C:\\Users\\Eliott\\Documents\\GitHub\\math\\01-canon\\theorems\\THM-534-lrc-sector-moment-lp-dual-certificate.md`\n- `C:\\Users\\Eliott\\Documents\\GitHub\\math\\05-knowledge\\hypotheses\\HYP-2052-lrc-loneliness-spectral-gap.md`\n- `C:\\Users\\Eliott\\Documents\\GitHub\\math\\05-knowledge\\hypotheses\\HYP-2084-lrc-second-gap-floor-tight-transversal.md`\n- `C:\\Users\\Eliott\\Documents\\GitHub\\math\\05-knowledge\\hypotheses\\HYP-2101-lrc-apex-lift-certificate-sheaf.md`\n- `C:\\Users\\Eliott\\Documents\\GitHub\\math\\07-reflections\\the-lrc-loneliness-spectral-gap-doubled-apex-s552.md`"
